Chapter: ShadowBuiltinFold

 A definition beats a builtin. `emit-compound` has always implemented
 that -- it checks `user-arity < 0` before routing a call to
 `emit-builtin` -- but the constant folder decided the same question
 separately and decided it the other way, so a shadowed name answered
 TWO ways in one function depending on the shape of its argument.

 `text-length "abc"` has a literal argument, so `fold-apply` rewrote it
 to `3` at lowering time using the compiler's own `text-length`, and the
 definition below never ran. `text-length (substring "abcde" 0 3)` has a
 computed argument, no fold applies, and the definition answered 99. Same
 name, same body, same function, two answers.

 It was first recorded as per-BUILTIN -- `text-length` shadowed
 and `abs` did not -- which is why it read as a property of how each
 builtin lowers. It is per-CALL-SITE. `abs` looked well-behaved only
 because nothing folds `abs`; give `text-length` a non-literal argument
 and it was well-behaved too.

 So the test needs BOTH call sites and it needs them in one program.
 Either one alone passes against the broken compiler: the computed
 argument was always right, and the literal one is right the moment the
 fold declines. Only the pair shows that the two paths agree, which is
 the actual claim.

 `abs` is here as the control. It was never folded and never wrong, so
 if it ever changes, the fix went too far and started displacing
 builtins that nobody shadowed.

Section: Entry

  text-length : Text -> Integer
  text-length (s) = 99

  abs : Integer -> Integer
  abs (n) = 77

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("lit " & show (text-length "abc"))
    print-line-uni ("var " & show (text-length (substring "abcde" 0 3)))
    print-line-uni ("abs " & show (abs (0 - 5)))
  end
